'''Marcos Aldana''' ({{circa}} 1898 – 14 February 1959) was a Black Balboan novelist, short story writer, and journalist whose work depicted the experience of Black Balboan migration from the [[Balboa|Balboan interior]] to [[Zong Balboa]]. His 1931 novel ''[[The Crossing (Aldana novel)|The Crossing]]'' is regarded as the foundational work of Black Balboan literature and one of the most significant novels produced in the Zong Balboa territory. Aldana spent the last two decades of his life in [[Huwei]], in the [[Kingdom of Zong]], where he continued to write and where he became a central figure in the Lurandian Zong literary community.

== Early life ==

Aldana was born in approximately 1898 in a rural community in the Balboan interior. The precise location is not documented; Aldana himself gave varying accounts in later interviews, describing it only as "a cotton place, two days' walk from the provincial capital." His parents were plantation labourers, and his childhood was spent in the conditions that he would later depict in the opening chapters of ''The Crossing'': nominal wages, restricted movement, corporal punishment, and the casual authority of the Balboan overseer.

Aldana learned to read from discarded newspapers and from the labels on shipping crates that passed through the plantation's supply depot. He received no formal education. In a 1934 interview with the ''[[New Horizon (Zong Balboa)|New Horizon]]'', he described his early literacy as "an accident of the trade — the plantation received goods in printed boxes, and I read the boxes because there was nothing else to read."

In 1914, at the age of approximately sixteen, Aldana migrated to [[Sant Cristòfor]] in [[Zong Balboa]]. The circumstances of his departure are not fully documented; he may have been among the early wave of Black Balboan migrants drawn by the Lîm Bûn-cheng administration's legal reforms. He made the border crossing on foot, a journey that he later fictionalised in ''The Crossing''.

== Life in Sant Cristòfor (1914–1935) ==

Aldana's first years in Sant Cristòfor were spent in the canal zone, where he found employment as a labourer on the canal maintenance crews. The work was physically demanding and poorly paid, but it provided a wage and a legal status that would have been impossible in the Balboan interior. By 1917, he had moved from the canal zone to the city itself, where he secured a position as a typesetter at the ''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'', the Volisan-language daily.

The typesetting position gave Aldana access to the printed word in a professional capacity and brought him into contact with the Volisan-language literary tradition. He learned to read Volisan during this period and began to read the Zong-language publications that circulated in the commercial district. His proximity to the editorial operations of the ''Faro'' — and to the journalists, printers, and writers who passed through its offices — provided an informal education in literary craft and in the political dynamics of the territory.

Aldana began writing fiction in the early 1920s. His first published story, "The Box" ("La Caja"), appeared in the ''[[New Horizon (Zong Balboa)|New Horizon]]'' in 1922. The story — a brief account of a plantation worker who teaches himself to read from the labels on shipping crates — drew directly on Aldana's own experience and established the autobiographical vein that would run through all of his subsequent work. Over the following decade, he published approximately twenty short stories in the ''New Horizon'' and, occasionally, in the ''[[Seng-kì-pò]]'' and the ''[[Chương Châu Nhật Báo]]''.

The stories of the 1920s depicted the lives of Black Balboan migrants in Sant Cristòfor: the conditions of the exurbs, the labour of the canal zone, the small dignities of community life, and the persistent gap between the territory's legal equality and its material inequality. They were written in Balboan Creole, a choice that was both practical — it was the language Aldana spoke — and political: the Zong literary establishment regarded Creole as a spoken dialect, and Aldana's insistence on writing in it was a declaration that the Black Balboan experience was a subject worthy of literature.

=== The Crossing ===

{{main|The Crossing (Aldana novel)}}

Aldana began writing ''The Crossing'' in 1928, working in the evenings after his shifts at the ''Faro''. The novel was completed in 1930 and published in 1931 by [[Portnou Press]]. The work brought Aldana a degree of recognition that his short stories had not achieved: it was reviewed in all four of the territory's principal newspapers, it was banned in Balboa, and it was translated into Zong and Volisan within three years of publication.

The novel's success did not substantially alter Aldana's material circumstances. He continued to work as a typesetter, and he continued to live in the harbour-front district rather than the western quarter. The modest income from the novel — Portnou Press's royalty structure was not generous — allowed him to reduce his hours at the ''Faro'' and to devote more time to writing, but he did not achieve the financial independence of the Zong literary figures with whom he was occasionally compared.

In 1926, Aldana married Chen Su-lêng (陳素瓊), a Zong woman who worked as a schoolteacher in the Sant Cristòfor public school system. The marriage was unusual for the period: interracial unions were not prohibited under the territory's anti-discrimination statutes, but they were sufficiently uncommon to attract comment in both the Zong and Black Balboan communities. The couple had two children, including a daughter, [[Chen Hui-lân Aldana]] (born 1928), who later became the first mixed-race member of the [[Parliament of Zong]]. The family lived in the harbour-front district, in a modest apartment that was better served than the exurbs but far removed from the western quarter.

=== The Exurb ===

Aldana's second novel, ''[[The Exurb]]'', was published in 1938. The work is set entirely in the Sant Cristòfor exurbs and depicts a single day in the life of a Black Balboan neighbourhood, from the early-morning departure of the canal workers to the evening gatherings in the communal courtyards. The novel's structure — a single day, a single place, a community observed in its routines — was influenced by the Zong literary tradition of the "day novel" (日記小說, ''ji̍t-kì sió-soat''), which Aldana had encountered through the Zong-language publications he read at the ''Faro''.

''The Exurb'' was published in the opening years of the [[Great Adonian War]], after the canal zone had been lost and the siege of Sant Cristòfor had begun. The novel's depiction of the exurbs — the informal settlements, the rudimentary sanitation, the community networks — acquired a retrospective poignancy that it could not have possessed at the time of writing. The edition was small, and many copies were destroyed during the siege. The work was not reprinted until 1955 and is accordingly less well-known than ''The Crossing'', though some critics regard it as the more accomplished novel.

== Departure from Sant Cristòfor ==

Aldana departed Sant Cristòfor in 1935, shortly after the outbreak of the [[Balboa Campaign]] and the beginning of the siege. The circumstances of his departure have been the subject of subsequent debate. Aldana himself, in later interviews, described it as a pragmatic decision: he was a known literary figure whose work had been banned in Balboa, and the Volisan and Balboan forces encircling the city were unlikely to treat a Black Balboan writer sympathetic to the Zong administration with leniency. His wife's Zong citizenship and the family's connections to the Zong literary establishment provided the practical means to secure passage on a Zong naval vessel that was evacuating civilians from the harbour before the siege lines closed. The family — Aldana, Chen Su-lêng, and their two children — departed together.

The decision to leave has been characterised by some later critics as an abandonment of the community that Aldana's work had documented. The charge is not without force: the Black Balboan population of the exurbs, which lacked the resources and institutional access to depart, remained in the city throughout the siege and bore the brunt of the famine and bombing. Aldana, who had the connections of a literary figure and the practical assistance of the Zong naval evacuation, was able to leave. He did not return.

Aldana addressed the question directly in a 1948 essay, "The Writer and the Siege," published in a Huwei literary journal:

{{quote|I wrote about the crossing. I did not expect to make it again. The people I wrote about could not leave. I could. I left. I have no defence and I offer none. The work is what remains.}}

== Life in Huwei (1935–1959) ==

Aldana settled in Huwei, the capital of the [[Kingdom of Zong]], where he was received as a significant literary figure. The Zong literary establishment, which had reviewed ''The Crossing'' with respectful distance in 1931, now treated him as a representative of the lost cosmopolitan culture of Sant Cristòfor. He was offered a position as a lecturer in Lurandian literature at the [[University of Huwei]], which he held from 1936 to 1946.

The Huwei years were Aldana's most productive period as a writer, though the work he produced was markedly different from the Sant Cristòfor fiction. The novels and stories of this period are characterised by a shift from the direct realism of ''The Crossing'' to a more reflective, essayistic mode: the experience of exile, the memory of the city, and the question of the writer's responsibility to the community he has left became the dominant subjects.

=== Remedios and Others ===

Aldana's third and final novel, ''[[Remedios and Others]]'', was published in 1946. The work returns to the border crossing of ''The Crossing'' but tells the story from the perspective of Remedios, the veteran border guide who escorted the Aldava family. The novel expands the crossing sequence into a full-length narrative, following Remedios over a decade of guiding migrants across the frontier. Each chapter describes a different crossing, and the accumulated effect is a portrait of the migration as a sustained, communal phenomenon rather than a single family's experience.

The novel was written in Huwei and is, in part, a meditation on distance: Aldana was writing about a border he could no longer cross, about a journey he had made in one direction only, and about a community he had left behind. The Zong critic [[Lîm Tek-kian]], who had written the most influential early assessment of ''The Crossing'', described ''Remedios and Others'' as "the work of a man writing from the far side of his own life, looking back at the threshold he crossed and seeing it whole."

=== Short stories and criticism ===

In addition to the three novels, Aldana published two collections of short stories in Huwei: ''[[The Canal and Other Stories]]'' (1941) and ''[[Letters from the Western Quarter]]'' (1951). The first collection drew on his memories of the Sant Cristòfor canal zone; the second was a series of epistolary stories set in the Zong commercial district, narrated from the perspective of a Black Balboan clerk working in a Zong merchant's office — a figure recognisably related to Dolores Aldava of ''The Crossing''.

Aldana also produced a substantial body of literary criticism, writing in Zong for Huwei's literary journals. His critical essays addressed the Zong literary tradition, the Volisan colonial novel, and the emerging post-war literature of the [[Adonian Community]] mandate territories. His most influential critical work, the essay "The Language of the Crossing" (1943), argued that the Balboan Creole spoken by the Black Balboan migrants was not a dialect of Volisan but a distinct literary language, and that its suppression by both the Balboan state and the Zong administration was an act of cultural erasure. The essay was cited in the post-war period as a foundational text in the movement for Creole-language literary recognition.

== Death ==

Aldana died on 14 February 1959 in Huwei, at the age of approximately sixty-one. The cause of death was reported as respiratory failure following a prolonged illness. He had never returned to Sant Cristòfor and had not revisited the Balboan interior since his departure in 1914. He was buried in the Huwei Municipal Cemetery, in a section reserved for foreign residents. A memorial plaque was installed at the [[Sant Cristòfor Academy]] site in 1965, during the Adonian Community mandate period, by a committee of former Black Balboan residents.

== Style and themes ==

Aldana's fiction is characterised by a direct, unadorned prose style, a commitment to Balboan Creole as a literary language, and a sustained attention to the material conditions of the Black Balboan community in Zong Balboa. His work is consistently realist in mode: he does not employ the lyrical experimentation of his Zong contemporary [[Kò͘ Chheng-hông]] or the allegorical strategies of the Volisan colonial novel. His sentences are short and concrete, his descriptions are specific, and his dialogue reproduces the rhythms of Creole speech.

The central theme of Aldana's work is the gap between legal equality and material inequality. The Zong territory's anti-discrimination statutes and universal suffrage provisions are treated as genuine achievements — Aldana does not dismiss them — but as insufficient to address the economic structures that reproduce racial and class hierarchies. The family in ''The Crossing'' gains the right to vote but not the right to housing; the community in ''The Exurb'' is legally equal but materially destitute; the migrants in ''Remedios and Others'' cross into a jurisdiction that affirms their humanity but cannot feed them.

A secondary theme, particularly in the Huwei period, is the writer's relationship to the community he depicts. Aldana's departure from Sant Cristòfor — and his subsequent refusal to return — produced a body of work that is simultaneously a document of the Black Balboan experience and a confession of the writer's distance from it. The later novels and essays are marked by a self-consciousness about this distance that is absent from the earlier, more confident fiction.

